# HeyGen Voice Script Rules

## Hard Constraints

- **Duration: up to 50 seconds** when read at HeyGen's natural pace (~150 wpm).
  - Target word count: **110–125 words max** (safe ceiling for 50s).
- **Format: a single paragraph.** No bullets, no numbered lists, no line breaks inside the script.
- **Tone: storytelling + humanistic.** Must NOT sound like AI.

## Writing Style

- Open with a hook (question, bold claim, or "imagine if…") within the first sentence.
- Use contractions ("you're", "it's", "don't") — feels human.
- Vary sentence length: mix short punchy lines with one longer flowing sentence.
- Use sensory or emotional words ("felt", "noticed", "shocked", "tiny", "quiet").
- End with a payoff or call-to-curiosity (NOT a generic "subscribe" line unless asked).

## Avoid

- Robotic phrasing: "In this video we will discuss…", "Today I am going to…", "Furthermore", "Moreover".
- Lists read out loud ("Number one… number two…").
- Jargon without a quick plain-language unpack.
- Repeating the title verbatim.

## Example (Good)

> Ever wonder why your phone battery dies twice as fast after a year? It's not aging — it's a tiny lie your phone tells you. The battery is fine; the software just stops trusting it. I tested this on three old phones and the result genuinely surprised me. One simple reset, and two of them gained back almost a full hour of screen time. Most people throw their phone away before trying it. Try it tonight before you spend a thousand bucks on a new one.

(~95 words, ~45s at 150 wpm — within budget.)
